      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed FeCoNiIr alloy can be processed as a homogeneous, single-phase fcc material.",
      "field": "conditions; boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "An equiatomic four-component FeCoNiIr composition has configurational entropy of at least 1.0R.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Arc melting or sputtering followed by annealing can avoid phase separation in FeCoNiIr.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ir has stronger spin–orbit coupling than both Pd and Pt.",
      "field": "hypothesis; mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Introducing Ir induces local lattice distortions in FeCoNiIr.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Introducing Ir may induce short-range chemical ordering in FeCoNiIr.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ir-induced spin–orbit coupling, lattice distortion, or short-range ordering alters the magnetocrystalline energy landscape.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Replacing Pd or Pt with Ir increases the magnetocrystalline anisotropy of the FeCoNi-based alloy.",
      "field": "hypothesis; mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Enhanced magnetocrystalline anisotropy more effectively pins domain walls.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "More effective domain-wall pinning raises the coercive field.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Equiatomic FeCoNiIr will have higher coercivity than equivalently processed FeCoNiPd and FeCoNiPt.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Equiatomic FeCoNiIr will retain saturation magnetization similar to that of FeCoNiPd and FeCoNiPt.",
      "field": "mechanism; predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The coercivity enhancement applies only while FeCoNiIr remains homogeneous, nearly equiatomic, and single-phase fcc.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Secondary phases or significant compositional inhomogeneity may negate the proposed coercivity enhancement.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The magnitude of the coercivity increase may vary with processing and microstructure.",
      "field": "uncertainty",
